.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CLIENT\-TRANSPORTS" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The bash\-server client bindings support four transport modes for
connecting to a bash\-server instance.
Each transport provides the same line\-oriented read/write interface to
the protocol layer, but differs in how the underlying connection is
established, where it is available, and what security properties it
provides.
.PP
This page describes each transport mode from the client perspective,
including when to use it, how to connect in each language, platform
availability, and security considerations.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Transport abstraction
All four client bindings implement a \f[CR]Transport\f[R] abstraction
(interface, abstract class, or protocol) that provides:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]connect()\f[R] \(en Establish the connection.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]read_line()\f[R] / \f[CR]readLine()\f[R] \(en Read one NDJSON
line.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]write()\f[R] \(en Write an NDJSON line.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]close()\f[R] \(en Tear down the connection.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]is_open\f[R] / \f[CR]isOpen\f[R] / \f[CR]isOpen()\f[R] \(en Check
connection status.
.PP
The \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] class accepts any transport and is agnostic to
the underlying connection mechanism.
This design allows switching transports without changing application
logic.
.SH Unix domain socket
\f[B]When to use:\f[R] Production deployments on Linux, macOS, and
Cygwin.
This is the default and recommended transport.
.PP
\f[B]How it works:\f[R] The client connects to an \f[CR]AF_UNIX\f[R]
stream socket at a filesystem path.
The server listens on this path after creating the socket file with
restricted permissions (mode 0700 directory, 0600 socket).
.PP
\f[B]Socket path discovery:\f[R]
.PP
The server determines the socket path using a resolution chain.
Clients should follow the same chain to find the server:
.IP "1." 3
CLI \f[CR]\-\-socket\f[R] argument (explicit path)
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]$BASH_SERVER_SOCKET\f[R] environment variable
.IP "3." 3
\f[CR]socket=\f[R] in \f[CR]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
\f[CR]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bash\-server/sock\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
\f[CR]/tmp/bash\-server\-<uid>/sock\f[R] (fallback)
.PP
Clients typically receive the socket path from the environment or a
configuration file.
.PP
\f[B]Connection examples:\f[R]
.PP
Python:
.IP
.EX
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq)
.EE
.PP
TypeScript:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);
.EE
.PP
C:
.IP
.EX
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Java:
.IP
.EX
BashClient client = BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Security:\f[R] The Unix socket is protected by filesystem
permissions.
Only the owning user (uid match) can connect.
The server optionally uses \f[CR]SO_PEERCRED\f[R] to verify the
peer\(cqs uid matches the server\(cqs uid (disable with
\f[CR]\-\-no\-peercred\f[R] for Python client compatibility on some
systems).
After connecting, clients must still authenticate with the server token.
.PP
