.SH File descriptor transport
\f[B]When to use:\f[R] Container and systemd integration, or any
scenario where a parent process pre\-opens the connection and passes it
to the client process via file descriptor inheritance.
.PP
\f[B]How it works:\f[R] The client receives an already\-connected file
descriptor (typically via \f[CR]fork()\f[R]/\f[CR]exec()\f[R] with the
fd kept open, or via systemd socket activation).
The client wraps this fd in a transport and communicates directly.
.PP
This is useful in orchestration scenarios:
.IP \(bu 2
A supervisor process connects to bash\-server and passes the fd to a
worker process.
.IP \(bu 2
systemd opens the socket and passes it to the service via
\f[CR]$LISTEN_FDS\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A container runtime pre\-opens the connection.
.PP
The server side uses \f[CR]\-\-fd N\f[R] to accept connections on an
inherited file descriptor rather than creating its own socket.
.PP
\f[B]Connection examples:\f[R]
.PP
Python:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] os
fd = int(os.environ[\(dqBASH_SERVER_FD\(dq])
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect_fd(fd)
.EE
.PP
TypeScript:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] fd = parseInt(process.env.BASH_SERVER_FD!, 10);
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connectFd(fd);
.EE
.PP
C:
.IP
.EX
int fd = atoi(getenv(\(dqBASH_SERVER_FD\(dq));
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect_fd(fd);
.EE
.PP
Java:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Java does not have a built\-in FdTransport factory method\f[R]
\f[I]// on BashClient. Use the FdTransport class directly:\f[R]
FdTransport t = \f[B]new\f[R] FdTransport();
t.connect(Integer.parseInt(System.getenv(\(dqBASH_SERVER_FD\(dq)));
BashClient client = \f[B]new\f[R] BashClient(t);
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Security:\f[R] Security depends on how the fd was obtained.
The fd itself provides no authentication \(en the caller must still send
an \f[CR]auth\f[R] message with the server token.
The fd is private to the process that received it (not accessible to
other processes unless explicitly shared via \f[CR]SCM_RIGHTS\f[R]).
.PP
