.SH Named Pipe transport
\f[B]When to use:\f[R] Windows and Cygwin environments where Unix domain
sockets are not available or not preferred.
Named Pipes are the native IPC mechanism on Windows.
.PP
\f[B]How it works:\f[R] The client connects to a Windows Named Pipe at a
path like \f[CR]\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsbash\-server\-<name>\f[R].
The server creates the pipe with a DACL (Discretionary Access Control
List) that restricts access to the pipe owner.
.PP
Named Pipes on Windows provide built\-in impersonation and access
control.
The Cygwin implementation maps between Cygwin and Windows paths
transparently.
.PP
The server uses \f[CR]\-\-named\-pipe [NAME]\f[R] to create a Named Pipe
instead of (or in addition to) a Unix socket.
The default pipe name is derived from the server name or the user\(cqs
SID.
.PP
\f[B]Pipe name discovery:\f[R]
.PP
The pipe name follows the same resolution pattern as the socket path:
.IP "1." 3
CLI \f[CR]\-\-named\-pipe NAME\f[R] argument
.IP "2." 3
Configuration file setting
.IP "3." 3
Default: \f[CR]\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsbash\-server\-<username>\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]Connection examples:\f[R]
.PP
Python:
.IP
.EX
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect_named_pipe(
    \(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq
)
.EE
.PP
TypeScript:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connectNamedPipe(
    \(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq
);
.EE
.PP
C:
.IP
.EX
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect_named_pipe(
    \(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq
);
.EE
.PP
Java:
.IP
.EX
BashClient client = BashClient.connectNamedPipe(
    \(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq
);
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Security:\f[R] Windows Named Pipes support DACLs for access
control.
The server creates the pipe with a DACL that allows only the creating
user to connect.
The token file is placed at a Windows\-accessible path for the client to
read.
Token authentication is still required after connecting.
.PP
