.PP
All transports require token\-based authentication after the connection
is established.
The transport\-level access control is a defense\-in\- depth measure
that restricts who can connect, while token authentication verifies the
client\(cqs identity.
.SH Custom transports
All bindings support creating a \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] from a
pre\-existing transport object.
This allows implementing custom transports (e.g., over TCP, SSH tunnels,
or message queues) by implementing the transport interface:
.PP
Python:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] MyTransport(Transport):
    \f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]def\f[R] connect(self, target): ...
    \f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]def\f[R] read_line(self) \-> str: ...
    \f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]def\f[R] write(self, data: str): ...
    \f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]def\f[R] close(self): ...
    \(atproperty
    \f[B]def\f[R] is_open(self) \-> bool: ...

transport = MyTransport()
\f[B]await\f[R] transport.connect(\(dqmy\-target\(dq)
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.from_transport(transport)
.EE
.PP
TypeScript:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] MyTransport \f[B]implements\f[R] Transport {
    \f[B]async\f[R] connect(target: string): Promise<void> { ... }
    \f[B]async\f[R] readLine(): Promise<string> { ... }
    \f[B]async\f[R] write(data: string): Promise<void> { ... }
    \f[B]async\f[R] close(): Promise<void> { ... }
    \f[B]get\f[R] isOpen(): boolean { ... }
}

\f[B]const\f[R] transport = \f[B]new\f[R] MyTransport();
\f[B]await\f[R] transport.connect(\(dqmy\-target\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] client = BashClient.fromTransport(transport);
.EE
.PP
C:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* The C binding does not support custom transports.\f[R]
\f[I]   Use bc_connect_fd() with a pre\-connected fd instead. */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Java:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]public\f[R] \f[B]class\f[R] MyTransport \f[B]implements\f[R] Transport {
    \f[B]public\f[R] void connect(String target) \f[B]throws\f[R] IOException { \f[B]...\f[R] }
    \f[B]public\f[R] String readLine() \f[B]throws\f[R] IOException { \f[B]...\f[R] }
    \f[B]public\f[R] void write(String data) \f[B]throws\f[R] IOException { \f[B]...\f[R] }
    \f[B]public\f[R] void close() \f[B]throws\f[R] IOException { \f[B]...\f[R] }
    \f[B]public\f[R] boolean isOpen() { \f[B]...\f[R] }
}

MyTransport t = \f[B]new\f[R] MyTransport();
t.connect(\(dqmy\-target\(dq);
BashClient client = \f[B]new\f[R] BashClient(t);
.EE
.SH Connection resilience
None of the bindings currently implement automatic reconnection.
If the transport connection is lost:
.IP \(bu 2
The reader loop/thread terminates.
.IP \(bu 2
Pending operations fail with \f[CR]TransportError\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]is_connected\f[R] / \f[CR]isConnected\f[R] property returns
\f[CR]false\f[R].
.PP
Applications that need reconnection should implement it at the
application level:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]def\f[R] resilient_eval(socket_path, token, command):
    \f[B]for\f[R] attempt \f[B]in\f[R] range(3):
        \f[B]try\f[R]:
            \f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]with\f[R] BashClient.connect(socket_path) \f[B]as\f[R] client:
                \f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token)
                \f[B]return\f[R] \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(command)
        \f[B]except\f[R] TransportError:
            \f[B]if\f[R] attempt == 2:
                \f[B]raise\f[R]
            \f[B]await\f[R] asyncio.sleep(1)
.EE
